# Shared TraceScene geometry tasks

Source: approved consolidation decision in the conversation. Upgrade TraceScene to serve the existing NearField tracing consumers, then remove the separate NearField geometry capture and GPU volumes. Preserve surface-cache capture/relighting, consumer-owned lighting algorithms, and WorldPartition residency ownership. World probes retain their existing scheduler.

## 1. Shared geometry and coverage contract

- [x] Inventory active geometry, lighting and material consumers; define a shared payload and sampling API covering known air, opaque geometry, unsupported geometry, unpublished/stale cells, material/emission and decoded light.
- [x] Define shared coverage and budgets: preserve the required 48-block near-field window and surface-cache tracing needs, share overlapping data, and justify which TraceScene levels are retained. Record formats, memory/upload costs and ownership before implementation.

Gate: every retained consumer has explicit data, coverage and validity requirements; no missing data is interpreted as empty geometry.

Contract and review: [shared geometry contract](LumOn.TraceSceneGeometryContract.md). Second review and independent completion audit passed. Delegated coverage/cost formula verification passed 333,216 assertions; no runtime implementation or performance result is claimed. Subsequent items implement this contract.

## 2. Shared worker capture and GPU publication

- [x] Adapt TraceScene to reuse worker-thread bulk capture and coalesced source snapshots; share source versions/invalidation and publish geometry, light, material references and readiness coherently.
- [x] Preserve WorldPartition as the residency owner, overlapping ring contents, bounded capture/upload work and stale-completion rejection. Keep old consumer wiring operational until the shared backend is verified.

Gate: controlled tests cover edits, missing chunks, unsupported shapes, movement, teleports and reused slots; no main-thread voxel capture or duplicate reads for the same shared source revision.

Evidence and traceability: [shared geometry contract](LumOn.TraceSceneGeometryContract.md), ownership and implementation acceptance; backend, publication and source-cache regression receipts summarized here. Second review and independent completion audit passed. Final combined validation passed 244 tests including 89 GPU cases; the final cache follow-up passed all 29 shared-backend cases. Production build passed with zero warnings/errors. Existing consumer wiring remains unchanged until item 3.

## 3. Switch tracing consumers

- [x] Switch screen-probe geometry tracing, hit lighting and world-probe visibility checks to the shared TraceScene resources. Preserve integer/fractional coordinate handling and hit/clear/unavailable/budget-exhausted outcomes.
- [x] Adapt surface-cache material capture and relighting to the shared sampling contract. Incomplete traces must not become successful clear segments or automatic sky contributions; preserve existing lighting evaluation for valid samples.

Gate: GPU regressions cover sealed rooms, doorways, missing/unsupported data, exhausted budgets, volume exits, camera motion and large coordinates, plus surface-cache capture/relight behavior.

Traceability for item 3:
- Screen/visibility switching -> [contract](LumOn.TraceSceneGeometryContract.md), Consumer inventory, Shared payload, Sampling and tracing API, Coverage and ring addressing -> one shared owner, independent logical domains, integer/fractional coordinates, explicit trace outcomes and material readiness -> `SharedTraceSceneScreenTests`, `LumOnDirectWorldProbeVisibilityTests.SharedDoorwayEditsAndCameraMovement`, and existing NearField/direct-visibility GPU regressions.
- Surface switching -> same contract, Shared payload, Sampling and tracing API, Ownership and implementation acceptance -> shared material capture, unchanged valid-hit shading, no sky/weight for incomplete rays, stale-history invalidation and retryable pages -> `SharedTraceSceneSurfaceTests`, existing surface GPU regressions, and `LumonSceneRelightBatchScheduleTests`.
- Runtime delivery -> same contract, Ownership and implementation acceptance -> shared bindings and regenerated shader assets -> production build with SPIR-V enabled and current-source GPU receipts. Legacy implementation removal, complete diagnostic updates and measured costs remain item 4.

Second review found and corrected partial-batch retry starvation, logical-domain history invalidation, signed ring wrapping and missing explicit compute bindings. A bounded runtime traversal loop resolved a driver instruction limit; the complete GLSL visibility run passed 76 tests (`artifacts/shared-consumers/final-visibility-fixed.trx`). The user subsequently required SPIR-V execution rather than a GLSL workaround for stale binaries. Compute tests now require compiled assets, and normal test builds regenerate and copy them. Missing-output regeneration, shared-include invalidation and rejection of disabled SPIR-V builds have passed (`artifacts/shared-consumers/spirv-*-proof.txt`). The final normal-build run passed all 28 strict SPIR-V compute tests with no skips (`artifacts/shared-consumers/compute-spirv-final.trx`); focused independent source review found no remaining compute blocker. The existing shader build covers compute stages only; graphics verification uses the existing GLSL path. The user accepted deferring shader-cost work to finish this item; extending graphics SPIR-V remains separate work. The matched worktree comparison records the link-time regression in `artifacts/visibility-comparison/comparison.md`; no rendering-performance improvement is claimed. Final current-source screen verification passed 91/91 cases (89 GPU and two scheduling tests), with no skips (`artifacts/shared-consumers/final-screen-current.trx`). Backend verification passed 131 cases in `final-backend-regression.trx`; its three fixture failures were corrected and passed in `final-spirv-backend-corrections.trx` (14/14). Normal SPIR-V-enabled builds passed. The second review and independent completion audit confirmed both item 3 requirements and the GPU gate fully satisfied, with no remaining required evidence gaps. Item 4 retains legacy removal, diagnostic cleanup and resource/performance measurements.

## 4. Remove duplication and validate

- [x] Remove replaced NearField capture, storage, publication ownership and obsolete TraceScene companion-payload plumbing. Update bindings, diagnostics and architecture records; retain the NearField Geometry viewer using the shared data.
- [x] Run builds and relevant regressions through a validation subagent. Compare source captures, uploads, resident memory and frame-thread work against the pre-change baseline for startup, movement and edits.

Gate: one shared geometry pipeline serves the retained consumers, no obsolete backend remains active, and resource/performance differences are recorded without conflating requested coverage with readiness.

Traceability for item 4:
- Backend retirement and diagnostics -> [contract](LumOn.TraceSceneGeometryContract.md#implemented-ownership-and-diagnostics), consumer inventory, payload, coverage and ownership -> one WorldPartition geometry registration and one capture/publication pipeline, retained NearField logical viewer, requested/readiness separation -> removed legacy production backends and migrated GPU fixtures; `TraceGeometryRenderer`, shared debug shaders, World Cell Bounds and profiling counters.
- Validation and costs -> [contract cost comparison](LumOn.TraceSceneGeometryContract.md#controlled-implementation-cost-comparison), resource ceilings and implementation acceptance -> controlled startup/movement/edit captures, executed upload bytes, actual nominal GL storage, separate source/staging/worker allocations and frame-thread work -> reusable `SharedGeometryCostMeasurementsTests`, `SharedGeometryWorkerCostMeasurementsTests`; baseline `artifacts/geometry-costs/legacy-cost-verified.trx` (2/2) and current `current-cost-asserted.trx` (5/5). Independent expected demand, complete readiness, exact captures and no duplicate key/revision requests are asserted.

Second review corrected fixture material-table generation reuse and removed leftover queue tokens and mapping setters. Shader-specific output cleanup was verified during validation, then removed at the user's request; generic stale-output handling remains part of item 5. Measurement exposed and fixed zero-budget secondary-cache retention; both disabled and bounded-cache regressions pass. The shared allocation increases default texture storage by 9,781,950 bytes; cumulative startup frame-thread allocations remain substantial. Synthetic timings do not establish live-game frame-rate or startup improvements.

Delegated regressions: `artifacts/geometry-cleanup/focused-cpu-compute.trx` passed 129/129 including 28 strict SPIR-V compute cases. `diagnostics-screen.trx` passed all 99 screen/material/compute cases; 24 viewer cases initially failed a missing shader variable and then passed in `diagnostics-fixed.trx` (30/30), alongside six WorldCell contract tests. The additional `bounds-payload.trx` run passed 3/3. Production/test builds passed and the retired scatter source/binary are absent from both output asset trees. The migrated visibility fixture passed 6/6 (`artifacts/geometry-costs/visibility-selected.trx`): doorway edits/movement and camera bob at a large negative origin, each through debug, SH4 and SH9 paths. Graphics still use the existing GLSL path; all-stage SPIR-V conversion belongs to item 5.
Independent completion audit re-read the task, controlling contract, implementation and final receipts. Both item 4 requirements and the gate are fully satisfied, with no remaining required issue or evidence gap.

## 5. Extend SPIR-V to all shader stages

- [x] Inventory all shader stages and program variants used by production and tests, including vertex, fragment, geometry and compute; include tessellation stages if present. Define deterministic variant identities for compile-time defines, configuration and generated sources, and explicit inter-stage locations/resource bindings that do not depend on optional SPIR-V debug names.
- [x] Extend `ShaderBuildTool` and build/package targets to compile every required stage and variant. Track source, include, compiler and variant-definition dependencies; regenerate changed or missing binaries before tests run, copy newly generated outputs, and prevent stale variants from being selected or packaged.
- [x] Switch graphics runtime loading and GPU test helpers to built SPIR-V assets, preserving specialization, program linking, resource bindings and reload behavior. Retain compute coverage and remove silent GLSL fallback; missing variants, invalid binaries or unsupported SPIR-V must produce explicit failures.
- [x] Validate clean and incremental builds, source/include/define changes, missing outputs, packaged assets and all used stage combinations through a validation subagent. Run rendering and visibility regressions through SPIR-V, verify that repeated draws do not compile GLSL, and record loading/specialization/linking timings separately from drawing costs.

Gate: every production and test shader stage/variant in the inventory is built and loaded through SPIR-V, normal test builds cannot silently reuse stale or missing assets, and rendering/binding regressions pass. SPIR-V conversion does not by itself establish lower driver linking cost; shader-cost optimization remains separate work.

Scope boundary: this work does not implement surface-cache hit-lighting integration into probes, migrate world-probe tracing to the GPU, change world-probe scheduling, or add multibounce lighting. Those remain separate work.

Item 5 implementation traceability:
- Inventory and variants -> item 5 contract above; approved owned-shader scope -> 38 vertex, 50 fragment and 10 compute entry points, including fourteen reusable test assets; no geometry or tessellation entry points currently exist. Base-game programs patched by VGE remain engine-owned and outside this conversion. Source-only preprocessor tests do not execute shaders. Runtime-generated test GLSL has been moved into named build inputs. `ShaderVariantSource` uses the runtime setting catalog, finite structural variants and typed numeric specialization constants; `GpuShaderContracts` contains the declarations moved from runtime layouts and is compiled into both runtime and build tool. `ShaderSourceLayout` applies contract-owned resource bindings and explicit interface locations to the AST before compilation. `SpirvInterfaceReflection` validates the compiler output and records runtime metadata; compiler binaries retain names and are published unchanged.
- Build/package -> item 5 contract above -> source/include and tool-content fingerprints, exact output hashes/set, required build producer, post-build enumeration/copy and generic orphan cleanup -> `ShaderBuildReceipt`, shader build project, production/test copy targets and Cake package overlay. Delegated build regression receipts passed, including canonical path equivalence, compiler native-content changes and removed input directories. The reusable regression is `ShaderBuildTool/Tests/ValidateBuildContract.ps1`. Actual copy targets removed seeded orphan binaries and manifests; actual Cake package verification passed 98 contracts, 243 variants and 590 dependency hashes (`artifacts/spirv-all-stages/contract-package-verification.txt`).
- Runtime/reload -> item 5 contract above -> manifest-selected binaries, integrity and current-source hashes, explicit failures, numeric binding lookup and lifecycle cleanup -> `SpirvStageLoader`, program-owned `GpuProgramInterface`, `GpuProgram.Spirv`, compute loading and test helpers. There is no production handle registry or temporary block-binding remapping. Compiled-contract layouts validate their slots and bind resources directly; they do not silently repair mismatched binaries. The earlier focused receipt passed 256/256 cases with no skips, including every one of the 243 built variants, shared-slot checks and both stale-source and contract-mismatch reload rollback (`artifacts/spirv-all-stages/contract-focused3.trx`). The complete GPU/inventory run passed 1,048 cases; its two obsolete-fixture failures were resolved as recorded below. Runtime block-member diagnostics use compiled offsets checked against active block sizes because the driver does not expose member/index associations for these SPIR-V programs. Functional rendering tests remain the evidence for actual buffer reads.
- Rendering and cost -> item 5 contract above; shared geometry contract's preserved sampling/visibility behavior -> existing rendering/visibility suites plus `SpirvGraphicsLifecycleTests`, separate read/binary-load/specialization/link/draw observations. Second source review and independent source review found no remaining implementation issue. Independent receipt review confirmed the broad rendering result and the test corrections. Current package and isolated build verification passed. The second review and independent completion audit confirmed all four requirements and the gate fully satisfied, with no remaining material issue or required evidence gap.

The earlier focused fixture records shader reads separately from binary upload and specialization: vertex 0.306 / 0.002 / 0.485 ms and fragment 0.432 / 0.001 / 0.285 ms, respectively. Linking plus numeric reflection took 2.838 ms; eight draws plus completion took 3.695 ms. These single-run fixture observations are not a game-performance benchmark or a claim of reduced shader cost.

The first broad SPIR-V run exposed a driver instruction-limit failure with explicitly specialized world-probe dimensions (levels/resolution 1/1, spacing 16, octahedral size/stride 16/2). The default-configuration inventory sweep alone did not cover this numeric specialization. Binary inspection identified 72 copies of the visibility traversal from eight explicit corner calls at nine consumer sites. The eight calls are now one SPIR-V no-unroll corner loop, preserving corner order, indices and weights; direction integration and traversal budgets are unchanged. Existing specialized sealed-room tests are the reproducer; all 10 targeted cases passed (`artifacts/spirv-all-stages/contract-visibility-fix2.trx`). The rebuilt binary contains nine traversal copies, and the full GPU run has no remaining driver instruction-limit failures.

Final rendering evidence: `artifacts/spirv-all-stages/contract-gpu2.trx` passed 1,048 cases, failed two outdated fixtures and retained three pre-existing static skips. The removed region-to-clipmap shader was referenced only by a legacy scatter integration test; that test was retired because current publication/capture/relight coverage lives in `SharedTraceSceneSurfaceTests`, with exact all-hit counters retained in `LumonSceneRelightVoxelDdaComputeTests`. The full-pipeline fixture now uploads the HZB source mip through its actual parameter buffer and supplies trace albedo/material inputs rather than obsolete direct-light inputs. All 17 shared-surface/relight cases passed in `contract-gpu-corrections.trx`; the remaining pipeline correction passed in `contract-pipeline-fix2.trx`. No production change was needed for these fixture failures. The unchanged skips are the obsolete SH4-versus-projection comparison and two legacy trace tint/distance tests; they do not represent missing SPIR-V assets or runtime skips. All 243 inventory variants executed successfully in the broad run.
Final build/package evidence: production/test build succeeded; the actual Cake Debug package passed directory and ZIP verification for 98 stage contracts, 243 variant hashes and 590 dependency hashes, with no orphan binaries/manifests, compiler temporary inputs or build receipt in the package (`artifacts/spirv-all-stages/contract-package.log`, `contract-package-verification.txt`). The final compiler passed isolated clean/incremental, equivalent-path, source/include/define, missing/corrupt/extra output, removed-source, tool/compiler fingerprint and package-integrity checks (`artifacts/spirv-all-stages/build-contract-probe/run-20260921-202832/receipts.json`, `result.txt`). The earlier reusable build-contract suite also verified native compiler content changes and removed input directories (`artifacts/spirv-build-contract/run-20260921-195301/receipts.json`). No live-game performance improvement is claimed.
Source-layout follow-up evidence: all 98 stages / 243 variants compiled with automatic location and binding assignment disabled. The normal test build passed; 264/264 focused cases passed, including every inventory variant, AST layout cases, compiled contract checks and lifecycle coverage (artifacts/spirv-all-stages/source-layout-focused.trx). The existing build-contract validator independently recompiled the same emitted fixture and confirmed byte-identical published SPIR-V, alongside its invalidation and isolated package checks (artifacts/spirv-build-contract/run-20260922-075525/result.txt). No live-game run was performed for this follow-up.

Contract-only loading follow-up: runtime manifests, offline binary reflection, and compiler-obedience binding checks have been removed. Shared stage contracts now own structural defaults, supported binary choices, conditional specialization declarations, and deterministic paths; shared binding contracts supply numeric resource lookup. Runtime loading reads only the selected SPIR-V asset, with no source-hash or manifest compatibility checks. Active-resource queries and ordinary OpenGL load/link diagnostics remain. Historical manifest/package receipts above describe the earlier implementation and do not establish validation of this follow-up.
Contract-only loading verification: normal builds produced 98 stages / 243 variants. The final broad selection passed 603 applicable cases covering every variant, active uniforms, direct-file compute, ring buffers, specialized world-probe visibility and graphics reload/disposal. Its sole other case expected the intentionally removed binding-mismatch rejection and was retired; seven focused loader and lifecycle cases then passed after rebuilding (artifacts/spirv-all-stages/no-manifest-post-review-focused.trx). Broad receipt: artifacts/spirv-all-stages/no-manifest-final-focused.trx. The latest build passed with five existing analyzer warnings and no errors (no-manifest-post-review-build.log). The isolated build-contract validator passed compiler-byte identity, invalidation and manifest-free package checks (artifacts/spirv-build-contract/run-20260922-100200/result.txt); the generated artifact tree contains zero runtime manifests (artifacts/spirv-all-stages/no-manifest-artifact-check.txt). No live-game validation was performed.
